WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.064
NIST's post-quantum cryptography effort used a multi-year public process to evaluate candidate algorithms.

2
00:00:07.064 --> 00:00:15.481
That process included security analysis, performance review, implementation considerations, and public cryptographic review.

3
00:00:15.481 --> 00:00:20.215
The open process matters because cryptographic confidence grows slowly.

4
00:00:20.215 --> 00:00:31.186
Algorithms need to survive review, attempted attacks, implementation experiments, and scrutiny from different communities before they are suitable for broad reliance.

5
00:00:31.186 --> 00:00:34.418
Security review is only one part of the evaluation.

6
00:00:34.418 --> 00:00:43.285
Algorithms also need acceptable performance, implementability, clear parameter choices, and behavior that can fit into real protocols.

7
00:00:43.285 --> 00:00:58.766
A strong algorithm on paper can still create deployment problems if keys are too large for constrained environments, signatures are too slow for common use cases, or implementations are difficult to make correct and resistant to side channels.

8
00:00:58.766 --> 00:01:02.749
The words selected and standardized are not interchangeable.

9
00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:13.194
A selected algorithm has been chosen for a path toward a standard, but the final document, naming, parameters, and implementation guidance may still be in progress.

10
00:01:13.194 --> 00:01:20.333
A standardized algorithm has a published standard that organizations and vendors can reference more directly.

11
00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:26.721
Migration programs should track both statuses so planning does not freeze at a single announcement.

12
00:01:26.721 --> 00:01:30.178
Organizations should also expect guidance to evolve.

13
00:01:30.178 --> 00:01:38.294
Standards, drafts, validation programs, protocol profiles, product support, and compliance expectations mature over time.

14
00:01:38.294 --> 00:01:43.404
A good PQC program watches the standards process, but it does not wait passively.

15
00:01:43.404 --> 00:01:52.722
It builds inventory, tests dependencies, asks vendors concrete questions, and designs cryptographic agility so future changes are manageable.
